**Android can't tell you who touched your phone. It keeps no list of who unlocked it, and it never takes a photo.** To find out, you need an app that uses the front camera when someone unlocks the phone or gets the PIN wrong. The best-known one is WTMP, short for "Who Touched My Phone", which saves a report on the phone each time. CrookCatcher takes a photo when someone enters a wrong PIN and emails it to you with the location. Which one fits depends on what you want: a record you read on the phone later, or a photo and location sent to you while the phone is out of your hands.

We make CrookCatcher, so read this with that in mind. What's said about WTMP comes from its own Google Play listing as it was on November 14, 2026. This guide is about protecting your own phone.

## What Android shows on its own

Not much. When someone enters a wrong PIN, Android rejects it and, after several tries, makes the person wait. It doesn't save the attempts anywhere you can look, and it doesn't use the camera. Here's [exactly what Android does with failed unlock attempts](/blog/failed-unlock-attempts-android).

If someone got in, there are traces: Digital Wellbeing counts how often the phone was unlocked and which apps were opened, and your browser and Google activity show what was done. See [how to tell if someone went through your Android phone](/blog/signs-someone-went-through-your-phone). None of it shows who it was.

## What the WTMP app does

WTMP — Who touched my phone? is made by Mindfulness Devs. It has more than 10 million downloads, and its listing was last updated in October 2026. This is how the listing describes it:

1. You open the app, press the button, and lock your phone.
2. When someone unlocks the phone, or tries to, the app starts a report: a photo from the front camera and the list of apps that were opened.
3. When the screen goes off, the report is saved.
4. You read the reports in the app, and you can sync them with the cloud.

The app uses Android's **Device administrator** permission to notice wrong unlock attempts. It's free to install, with in-app purchases.

Two things in that description matter:

- **It records correct unlocks too.** Its listing says each report includes a photo and the apps that were opened, even if the person knew your PIN.
- **The reports stay in the app.** The listing mentions no email alert and no location. You see what happened when you have the phone back in your hand.

## What CrookCatcher does differently

CrookCatcher reacts when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password. It takes a photo with the front camera, records the GPS location and an approximate address, and emails all of it to you. You don't need the phone to see it.

