## WTMP and CrookCatcher compared

| | WTMP | CrookCatcher |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Photo after a wrong PIN | Yes | Yes |
| Photo after a correct unlock | Yes | Only after a wrong attempt first (Pro) |
| List of apps that were opened | Yes | No |
| Email alert with the photo | Not mentioned | Yes |
| GPS location | Not mentioned | Yes |
| Where you see it | In the app, optional cloud sync | In the app and in your email |
| Price | Free, in-app purchases | Free, in-app purchases |

"Not mentioned" means the app's Play listing doesn't describe the feature.

## Which one should you use?

- **Someone else may know your PIN:** change it first, so it stops working for them. If you also want a record of correct unlocks on the phone, WTMP's listing says it does that.
- **You're thinking about theft, or about a phone left somewhere:** CrookCatcher, because the photo and location reach your email while the phone is gone.
- **Someone keeps guessing your PIN:** both do the job. CrookCatcher tells you right away by email; WTMP shows you the next time you open the app.

Whichever you choose, test it: lock your phone, enter a wrong PIN on purpose, unlock it and check that your own photo is there. Test again a week later, because some phones stop background apps after a few days.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: Can Android show who touched my phone?
A: No. Android has no log of who unlocked the phone and never takes a photo. Digital Wellbeing shows how many times the phone was unlocked, but not by whom.

### Q: Is the Who Touched My Phone app free?
A: WTMP is free to install, and its Google Play listing shows in-app purchases. CrookCatcher is also free to install; the photo, location and email alert are in the free version.

### Q: Is the WTMP app safe?
A: As of November 14, 2026, its Google Play data safety section states that no data is collected and none is shared with third parties. Like every app of this kind, it needs the Device administrator permission, which has to be switched off again before you can uninstall it.

### Q: How do I know if someone touched my phone while it was out of my sight?
A: Check Digital Wellbeing afterwards for unlocks you didn't make. For proof of who it was, install one of these apps beforehand, so the next attempt is photographed.

### Q: Do these apps work with fingerprint or face unlock?
A: You can keep using both. CrookCatcher reacts to a wrong PIN, pattern or password; a failed fingerprint or face attempt doesn't trigger a photo. WTMP's listing says it records when the phone is unlocked or an unlock is attempted.

### Q: Why didn't the app take a photo?
A: Most often the phone's battery saver stopped the app in the background, or the phone was just restarted. After a restart, Android only lets apps use the camera once the phone has been unlocked one time.

